---
// Window cleaning — a LEAF page under the Home Cleaning mega-menu's third
// group, "Outside your home" (navigation.ts). First of the five in that group
// to be built, alongside gutter, roof, high pressure and exterior house
// washing in the same pass.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTS for any of the five. Every page built from
// content-plans/ has a file there; this group has none. All copy below is
// DRAFTED, not supplied, and needs sign-off before launch. Where a claim
// depends on a fact only TLB holds it ships as a visible [bracket], per house
// convention, rather than as a plausible guess.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH:
//   - IS THIS DELIVERED IN HOUSE? Window cleaning needs its own kit: a
//     water-fed pole system with a filter trolley for anything above the
//     ground floor, and squeegees, scrims and a ladder for the rest. Nothing
//     in the brand foundation document or any existing page says TLB owns
//     any of it. If the work is subcontracted, §9's comparison table (row
//     one: "employed by TLB, not subcontracted") contradicts the page it
//     sits on and has to come off this page.
//   - HOW HIGH DO WE GO? §4 and branded FAQ 3 both say first-floor glass is
//     reached from the ground with a pole and that nothing needs a ladder on
//     a roof. Confirm that is true, and confirm the cut-off. A two-storey
//     home with a stairwell window over a paved courtyard is the job that
//     tests it, and it is common right through Byron and Lennox.
//   - PRICING BASIS. Per window, per pane or per hour, and the minimum
//     charge. Bracketed in non-branded FAQ 5, which is the most searched
//     question in this category.
//   - INSIDE AS WELL AS OUT? Bracketed in branded FAQ 1. It changes the
//     price, the time on site and whether anyone needs to be home.
//   - SCREENS AND FLYSCREENS. Bracketed in §4. Most enquiries assume it.
//   - [insured] in §3 card 3 is inherited from the commercial hub, where the
//     same bracket is already open. One answer fixes every page.
//
// PHOTOGRAPHY: four generated assets of its own in
// src/assets/home_cleaning/outside_your_home/window_cleaning/, prompts in the
// IMAGE-PROMPTS.md beside them, plus the cluster's shared backdrop one level
// up. Same deliberate uniform deviation as the commercial pressure page: no
// apron on outdoor work. Argued in that folder's sheet and TLB's call to
// reverse.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import PathwayCards from '../../components/sections/PathwayCards.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import BeforeAfter from '../../components/sections/BeforeAfter.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../../components/sections/ComparisonTable.astro';
import GoogleReviewSlider from '../../components/sections/GoogleReviewSlider.astro';
import { googleBusinessProfileUrl, googleReviews } from '../../data/googleReviews';
import TagCloud from '../../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
import footerLogoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
// This page's own four frames. Nothing is reused from the indoor folders:
// IMAGE-GUIDELINES §5 cuts both ways, and an indoor scene cannot stand in for
// glass seen from the garden.
// The cluster's shared backdrop, one folder up: a clean coastal home exterior,
// no people, no equipment, generous open lawn across the lower third where the
// copy sits. All five "Outside your home" pages use this one file rather than
// five near-identical exteriors, which is §5's reuse rule applied honestly:
// same subject, same file.
// SAMPLE illustrations, not photographs. See §6.
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../../data/navigation';
import { comparisonColumns, comparisonRows } from '../../data/comparison';
import { locationGroups } from '../../data/locations';
import { trustBarCards } from '../../data/trust';
import { otherOutsideServices } from '../../data/outsideYourHome';
import { theLineHeading, howItWorks, managedForYou, theLineBody } from '../../data/brand-lines';
import { localBusiness } from '../../data/business';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized } from '../../lib/cms-schemas';

// §0 Metadata. Focus keyword leads the title, matching every sibling. "Window
// washing" and "window cleaner" are the common synonyms and are carried in
// body copy rather than the title, so the two do not read as two services.
// Title and description are the SIGNED-OFF workbook text (Keyword Mapping &
// Metadata, sheet 1 row 13), reproduced verbatim. The old title stopped one
// word short of the mapped primary `window cleaning services` (400/mo) and
// left 23 characters unused; the old description ran 12 characters over.
//
// The approved description trades the region for the two things people
// actually search on here — `water fed window cleaning` (200/mo) and
// `streak free window cleaning` (200/mo) — and closes on an instruction to act
// where the old one closed on a description. The salt film is not lost: it
// moves into the hero paragraph below.
//
// NO BLOCKING FLAGS on this page. The approved title, meta and CTA are all
// consistent with what the page says it does, which makes it the cleanest of
// the five exterior pages to action.
//
// In `pageMeta`, on `canonical`:
// ⚠️ Placeholder domain, same as every sibling page. Confirm before launch.

// §1 Hero. Kicker carries the focus keyword so someone arriving from a search
// result gets confirmation above the H1, which is then free to be a benefit.
// Same call as every service page here.
//
// The H1 now carries the mapped primary `window cleaning services` (400/mo) as
// an exact contiguous phrase, extended with the region: local intent on this
// row totals 1,450/mo across three terms, more than three times the mapped
// primary itself. The old heading — the thing this service actually sells,
// which is not clean glass but the view people paid for — is not cut. It opens
// the lead, because it is two short sentences that work by their rhythm.
//
// The subheading is a NEW line. Its extension is outcome plus scope rather
// than the H1's region, it carries `streak free window cleaning` (200/mo),
// which appeared nowhere on the page despite being in the approved title, and
// naming frames, sills and tracks above the fold answers the page's own "what
// is included besides the glass" objection before the reader has to ask it.
//
// A SCENE PHOTO, family A. A cut-out figure on a plain ground says "a
// cleaner" without saying windows; the house, the garden and the wet arc on
// the glass are what carry it, and none of them survives a cut-out.
// `imageFit` is unset, so Placeholder's `cover` default applies, and `ratio`
// is 1/1 because the source file is square, so the box crops nothing.
// The page's one primary CTA, per BFD 9.5, repeated on every in-page button,
// in place of the six competing labels this page carried — the most of the
// eight. `Book a window clean for your home` is the approved workbook CTA and
// it fits a button at 33 characters, so no split is needed.
//
// It also resolves a BFD 13.2 conflict: the old `Get an instant quote`
// promised instant pricing on a page whose own pricing block says the honest
// answer is a fast quote, and whose last FAQ says the rate moves with height,
// access and how many are sliding doors.
//
// The header keeps the site-wide quoteCta — that is chrome, not this page.
// Routing card 3 keeps `Set up your regular clean`, because it sends the
// reader to the regular cleaning page, which genuinely is instant-priced.
// That is navigation, not this page's CTA.

// §2 Definition. The answer-target block for the focus keyword and the first
// place the phrase appears in body copy. Paragraph two does the synonym work
// and, more usefully, names what makes glass here different from glass in
// Sydney: the salt. That is the fact this whole page turns on.

// §3 Pathway cards. The three ways people actually arrive at this page, in
// their own words. Same framing every service page here uses, re-pointed at
// glass.
//
// In `pathwayCards`, on the photo of "The glass has gone cloudy and I cannot fix it":
// No person in this frame on purpose: the subject is the film on the
// glass, and a figure would compete with it.
// In `pathwayCards`, on the photo of "I want it done with everything else":
// A visible, friendly face, because the copy beside it promises the
// same small team. A figure turned away would argue with its caption.
// In `pathwayCards`, on the description of "I want it done with everything else":
// ⚠️ [insured] inherited from the commercial hub, where the bracket is
// already open. One answer fixes every page.

// §4 What the job covers. The most important block on the page, for the same
// reason it is on the commercial pressure page: this site tells readers to
// demand a written scope and then has to show one.
//
// ⚠️ EVERY ITEM IS DRAFTED against ordinary practice, not supplied by TLB.
// Confirm each one and cut anything TLB does not actually do. Two carry their
// own blockers, marked inline.
//
// In `scopeItems`, on "Glass, both sides":
// ⚠️ BLOCKER. Inside as well as out is assumed here and bracketed in
// branded FAQ 1. It changes the price, the time on site and whether
// anyone has to be home.
// In `scopeItems`, on "Flyscreens and security screens":
// ⚠️ BLOCKER. Screens are the single most common assumption in an
// enquiry, and whether they come out of the frame or get washed in
// place is a real difference in time.

// §5 A note, not a claim. Callout `note` tone, the same job the commercial
// pressure page gives its "worth knowing" block: make the reader distrust a
// cheap quote without making a single claim about TLB. Here the lever is that
// coastal glass genuinely behaves differently, which is checkable and is the
// thing most competitor pages in this category never mention.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFY THE WORDING. Salt aerosol carried inland from surf is well
// documented and is the reason coastal glazing and window hardware carry
// their own maintenance advice from manufacturers. Stated here as behaviour
// people can see on their own windows, with no number attached, because the
// distance salt carries depends on the wind and the surf and any figure would
// be invented. If TLB wants a source on the page, glazing manufacturers'
// own care guidance is the honest one to cite.

// §6 Before and after. SAMPLE illustrations, not photographs.
//
// ⚠️ These are flat vector drawings generated by
// scripts/make-exterior-before-after-samples.mjs, each stamped with a visible
// SAMPLE badge, and their filenames say `sample-` for the same reason. A
// fabricated "before and after from a real job" is a false trust claim and
// IMAGE-GUIDELINES §7 forbids it. The `note` prop below tells the reader so
// in their own view.
//
// SWAPPING IN A REAL PAIR is two import lines and deleting the note. THE
// PHOTO BRIEF: one window, shot from a fixed tripod position, before and
// after, with nothing moved between the frames. Shoot from outside with the
// interior in shade so the glass reads as glass rather than as a mirror, and
// pick a pane with visible salt film rather than mud, because mud looks like
// a one-off and salt is the recurring sale. The component wipes one frame
// over the other in place, so a shifted camera reads as a glitch.

// §7 What to ask. Persuasive block, dark TextBlock, no claim about TLB in it.
// Two questions, both of which a competent operator answers straight away and
// a thin one talks around.

// §8 The rest of the cluster. Shared data from src/data/outsideYourHome.ts, so
// the five pages cannot drift into five different lists. This page's own href
// is filtered out.
const siblingItems = otherOutsideServices('/house-cleaning/window-cleaning/');

// §9 Comparison table. The shared rows from src/data/comparison.ts.
//
// ⚠️ CONDITIONAL, and it is this file's first launch blocker. Row one is
// "Nine full-time cleaners employed by TLB, not subcontracted". If window
// cleaning is subcontracted to an operator with the pole system, the table
// contradicts the page it sits on and has to come off this page. A question
// for TLB, not a rewrite.

// §11 Branded FAQs, in the customer's voice: "we" and "our" mean the reader's
// household, not TLB. That register is held across all five pages in this
// group. Each answer is a self-contained 40 to 80 word passage, per BFD 3.5.
//
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Do you do the inside of the windows as well?":
// ⚠️ BLOCKER. See §4.
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Can you reach our upstairs windows?":
// ⚠️ BLOCKER. Confirm the real cut-off. See the file header.

// §12 Non-branded FAQs. The long-tail block, aimed at what people search
// rather than what they ask us.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED:
//   - Pure water and water-fed poles. Standard industry practice, correct as
//     stated: filtered water dries without leaving mineral spots, which is
//     what removes the need to squeegee at height.
//   - Salt film behaviour. Observable and correct in substance. Deliberately
//     carries no distance figure. See §5.
//   - Hard water spotting etching glass over time is well established and is
//     why the answer distinguishes it from a film that will wash off.
//   - Frequency is an observation, not a rule, and is hedged.
//   - Pricing is bracketed on purpose. A "from $X per window" that turns out
//     to be wrong is worse than no number, and the ACCC treats from-pricing
//     as a claim.
//
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "How much does window cleaning cost?":
// ⚠️ BRACKETED ON PURPOSE. See the note above this array.

// §13 Closing CTA. Same offer as the hero, worded as what actually happens.

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/house-cleaning/window-cleaning.yaml and is
// edited in Pages CMS ("Window cleaning"). This file owns the layout. The notes
// above explain why each section says what it says; they stay here because
// Pages CMS rewrites the YAML on save and would drop them.
const page = await getPage(
  'house-cleaning/window-cleaning',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    // The text of the page's one primary button. It always links to the
    // site-wide quote page, so only the words are editable.
    buttonLabel: section.text,
    hero: section.hero,
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    // A card whose button is left empty gets the page's primary button.
    pathways: section.pathwayCards.extend({
      cards: z.array(section.pathwayCards.shape.cards.element.extend({ cta: section.optional(section.link) })).min(1),
    }),
    method: section.iconGrid,
    salt: section.callout,
    beforeAfter: section.beforeAfter,
    questions: section.textBlock,
    // The rows are the shared outside-your-home cross-links.
    otherServices: section.sectionIntro,
    // The columns and rows are the shared comparison table (Site settings).
    comparison: section.comparisonTable.omit({ columns: true, rows: true }),
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    whereWeClean: section.tagCloud,
    bookingFaq: section.faq,
    generalFaq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/house-cleaning/window-cleaning/',
};

const business = localBusiness({
  description: pageMeta.description,
  areaServed: 'Northern Rivers NSW, the Tweed and Southern Gold Coast QLD',
  sameAs: googleBusinessProfileUrl ? [googleBusinessProfileUrl] : [],
});

const pageCta = { ...quoteCta, label: page.buttonLabel };

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '1/1');

const pathwayCards = page.pathways.cards.map((card, i) => ({
  number: i + 1,
  ...card,
  image: sized(card.image, '4/3'),
  cta: card.cta ?? pageCta,
}));

---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? pageCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    {/* Trust bar and definition share ONE photo behind a Cream scrim, laid across
        both sections. 90%, raised from 75% on 29 Sep 2026. Measured over this
        frame's own darkest region under the copy column, 75% gave 4.57:1 — a
        bare pass with no margin over a photo. 90% gives 6.13:1.
        Backdrop.astro's `opacity` doc carries the method. `focus="bottom"`
        keeps the open lawn and paving the shot was composed for, rather than
        the roofline. */}
    <Backdrop image={page.definition.backgroundImage} tint="muted" opacity={90} focus="bottom">
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock
        theme="muted"
        align="left"
        heading={page.definition.heading}
        body={page.definition.body}
        cta={page.definition.cta}
      />
    </Backdrop>

    <PathwayCards heading={page.pathways.heading} headingAccent={page.pathways.headingAccent} cards={pathwayCards} />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="icon-grid"
      heading={page.method.heading}
      lead={page.method.lead}
      items={page.method.items}
      note={page.method.note}
      cta={pageCta}
    />

    <Callout tone="note" heading={page.salt.heading} body={page.salt.body} cta={page.salt.cta} />

    <BeforeAfter
      heading={page.beforeAfter.heading}
      lead={page.beforeAfter.lead}
      pairs={page.beforeAfter.pairs}
      note={page.beforeAfter.note}
      beforeLabel={page.beforeAfter.beforeLabel}
      afterLabel={page.beforeAfter.afterLabel}
    />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading={page.questions.heading}
      body={page.questions.body}
      cta={page.questions.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.questions.backgroundImage}
    />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="list"
      heading={page.otherServices.heading}
      lead={page.otherServices.lead}
      items={siblingItems}
    />

    <ComparisonTable
      heading={page.comparison.heading}
      cornerLabel={page.comparison.cornerLabel}
      lead={page.comparison.lead}
      columns={comparisonColumns}
      rows={comparisonRows}
      footnote={page.comparison.footnote}
      cta={page.comparison.cta}
    />

    <GoogleReviewSlider heading={page.reviews.heading} reviews={googleReviews} profileUrl={googleBusinessProfileUrl} />

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.whereWeClean.heading}
      subheading={page.whereWeClean.subheading}
      tags={page.whereWeClean.tags}
      groups={locationGroups}
      note={page.whereWeClean.note}
    />

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `fast` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={theLineHeading.fast}
      body={[managedForYou.oneOff, ...theLineBody.residential, ...howItWorks.general]}
    />

    {/* The first FAQ heading renamed: these five are about inside glass,
        frequency, upstairs reach, access and rain, all pre-booking questions,
        and it matches the carpet page's "Booking a carpet clean". "Window
        clean" rather than "window cleaning" keeps it out of Rule 5's count,
        which is already full at three — the H1, the hero subheading and the
        coverage H2. The second heading stays: it carries `window cleaning`
        (2,600/mo), the largest term on the row, and is not a fourth instance
        of the mapped primary because it does not contain "services". */}
    <Faq heading={page.bookingFaq.heading} lead={page.bookingFaq.lead} items={page.bookingFaq.items} />
    <Faq heading={page.generalFaq.heading} lead={page.generalFaq.lead} items={page.generalFaq.items} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={page.closingCta.kicker}
      heading={page.closingCta.heading}
      lead={page.closingCta.lead}
      cta={page.closingCta.cta ?? pageCta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
